Theorem IC-1: SCC decomposition + topological ordering of the call graph

  Preconditions:
    - CallGraph G = (V, E) is the directed call graph for a compilation
      unit; V = ArcFunction names; E = direct-call edges per
      ori_arc::graph::call_graph::CallGraph::build
    - G is well-formed: every src/dst Name in E resolves to a node in V
      OR to an external/FFI symbol skipped at SCC consumption time per
      Annex E §AIMS PL-1 + ori_arc::aims::interprocedural::
      analyze_program sec-7 step "External/FFI functions not in
      func_by_name are skipped"
    - IC-1 type signature (per Interprocedural.proof lines 16-19):
        scc_decompose : CallGraph -> List (List FunctionId)
      whose List output enumerates SCCs in FORWARD topological order
      (callees before callers) per Annex E §AIMS PL-1a + sec-5
      IC-1 "call graph decomposed into SCCs; processed in topological
      order (callees before callers)"
    - SCC = maximal set of mutually-reachable vertices under E; per
      Tarjan, "Depth-First Search and Linear Graph Algorithms" (SIAM
      J. Comput., 1972) every directed graph has a unique SCC partition
    - Topological order = linear extension of the condensation DAG
      G_scc = (V_scc, E_scc), where V_scc = SCC partition of V and
      E_scc has (S_i -> S_j) iff (u -> v) in E for some u in S_i,
      v in S_j, S_i != S_j; G_scc is acyclic by construction (Cormen,
      Leiserson, Rivest, Stein, "Introduction to Algorithms", 3rd ed.,
      sec-22.5)
    - IC-1 is the antecedent of every downstream IC rule: IC-2/IC-3
      parameter-contract fixpoint, IC-4 return-contract join, IC-5
      EffectSummary derivation, IC-6 FipContract tier join, IC-7
      convergence bound — all require the topological ordering of the
      SCC list to be sound per Annex E §AIMS + sec-7 PL-1a
    - Shipped engine: ori_arc::graph::scc::compute_sccs uses iterative
      Tarjan's strongconnect (compiler/ori_arc/src/graph/
      scc/mod.rs lines 48-152); IC-1 verifies the ALGORITHM CONTRACT,
      not the iterative-stack-frame mechanics

  Soundness property:
    Forall G in CallGraph (well-formed).
      Let sccs := scc_decompose(G) be the emitted SCC list.
      Three conjuncts SHALL hold:

      (P1) Partition correctness:
        sccs forms a partition of V(G):
          (a) every v in V appears in exactly one scc in sccs;
          (b) every scc in sccs is non-empty;
          (c) sccs are pairwise disjoint.

      (P2) SCC maximality:
        For every scc in sccs and every pair (u, v) in scc x scc:
          u reaches v in G AND v reaches u in G.
        For every pair (u, v) in V x V with u reaches v AND v reaches u:
          u and v are in the same scc.
        (Equivalently: each scc is a strongly-connected component
        under the standard reachability equivalence relation.)

      (P3) Forward topological ordering:
        For every pair of distinct SCCs (S_i, S_j) appearing at
        positions (i, j) in the list sccs with i < j:
          there exists NO edge (u, v) in E with u in S_j and v in S_i.
        (Equivalently: every cross-SCC edge (u in S_i, v in S_j) with
        S_i != S_j satisfies position(S_i) < position(S_j); callees
        appear before callers in the emitted list.)

  Proof obligation:
    Three-part discharge corresponding to (P1), (P2), (P3); each
    grounded in Tarjan's 1972 result + standard graph-theory texts.

    Part (P1) — Partition correctness:

      Tarjan's strongconnect (sec-3 of the 1972 paper, restated for
      the iterative implementation in ori_arc::graph::scc::mod.rs
      lines 100-152) maintains the following loop invariants
      throughout DFS over the unvisited frontier:

        (i) every vertex v in V is visited exactly once via the
              outer `for root in graph.nodes()` loop guarded by
              `if !state.indices.contains_key(&root)` (line 64);
        (ii) on entry to strongconnect(root), root is pushed onto
              the working stack `state.stack` and marked on_stack;
        (iii) on completion of root's DFS subtree, when root is the
              lowlink-equals-index "root of an SCC" sentinel, every
              vertex above root on the stack (including root itself)
              is popped into a fresh Scc.members vec.

      From (i) every v in V is visited; from (ii) every visited v is
      pushed onto the stack; from (iii) every stacked v is popped into
      exactly one Scc.members vec at the unique completion point of
      its containing SCC's root. Therefore every v in V appears in
      exactly one Scc.members vec — conjunct (a).

      The Scc.members vec is initialized in the pop loop (lines 140-
      147 of mod.rs) and the SCC is pushed onto state.sccs only after
      at least one vertex (root itself) is popped — Scc.members is
      non-empty — conjunct (b).

      Pairwise disjointness follows from (i) + (iii): a vertex popped
      into one Scc.members is removed from state.on_stack and never
      re-stacked, so it cannot appear in any subsequent pop loop —
      conjunct (c).

      (P1) discharged.

    Part (P2) — SCC maximality:

      Tarjan's strongconnect computes per-vertex `lowlink` =
      min(index of v, min over w reachable from v of index of w
      where w is on the working stack at the time of v's
      completion). The algorithm pops a vertex v's strongconnect
      frame and emits an SCC iff lowlink(v) == index(v) at frame
      completion.

      Tarjan (1972, Theorem 13) proves: lowlink(v) == index(v) at
      frame completion IFF v is the root of an SCC in the DFS tree,
      AND the set of vertices popped (all vertices above v on the
      stack, plus v) is exactly the SCC containing v under the
      reachability-equivalence relation.

      The shipped strongconnect in mod.rs lines 100-152 implements
      this lowlink + on_stack discipline directly:
        - line 110: index assignment via state.index counter
        - line 111-112: lowlink initialized to index
        - line 113-116: push onto state.stack + mark on_stack
        - line 122-132: on successor recursion return, lowlink
          updated to min(lowlink(v), lowlink(w)) when w is a tree
          edge, or min(lowlink(v), index(w)) when w is a back edge
          on the stack — the textbook Tarjan update rule
        - line 138-148: pop-into-SCC gate triggered iff
          lowlink(v) == index(v) at frame completion

      By Tarjan's Theorem 13 applied to this implementation:
        (P2-forward) Every Scc.members emitted contains exactly a
        maximal set of mutually-reachable vertices.
        (P2-backward) Every pair (u, v) with u reaches v AND v
        reaches u lands in the same Scc.members.

      (P2) discharged via Tarjan 1972 Theorem 13 + structural
      correspondence of the shipped iterative algorithm to the
      published recursive specification.

    Part (P3) — Forward topological ordering:

      Tarjan's algorithm emits SCCs at the moment their root vertex
      completes — i.e., when DFS retreats from the last unvisited
      descendant. This emission order has the canonical property
      (Tarjan 1972, sec-3 closing remarks; restated in CLRS 3rd ed.
      sec-22.5 Theorem 22.16):

        SCCs are emitted in REVERSE topological order of the
        condensation DAG.

      That is, if S_i and S_j are distinct SCCs and there exists a
      cross-SCC edge (u in S_i, v in S_j) with u reaches v and NOT
      vice versa (so position(S_i, S_j) in condensation), then S_j
      is emitted BEFORE S_i in Tarjan's natural output.

      The shipped compute_sccs in mod.rs lines 57-76 emits SCCs into
      state.sccs in this reverse-topological order (Tarjan natural).
      Lines 79-87 then document that this IS forward topological
      order from the CALLEE-FIRST perspective: leaf SCCs (no
      outgoing call edges, i.e., terminal sinks in the call graph)
      appear FIRST in the output vec, root SCCs (no incoming call
      edges, i.e., entry points / main) appear LAST.

      Specifically, the comment block at mod.rs lines 49-55 states:
        "Returns SCCs in **forward topological order**: leaf SCCs
         (no outgoing call edges) appear FIRST, root SCCs (no
         incoming call edges) appear LAST. This is the natural
         output of Tarjan's and the correct evaluation order for
         borrow inference — analyze leaf functions before their
         callers."

      The terminology agreement between Tarjan's "reverse
      topological" and this file's "forward topological" reduces to
      the orientation convention: in the call graph, an edge
      (caller -> callee) means caller depends on callee, so callees
      are PREDECESSORS in dependency order. Tarjan emits
      predecessors first (reverse topological of the edge
      direction = forward topological of the dependency order).
      This is exactly the order IC-1 requires per Annex E §AIMS IC-1 "callees before callers".

      Formally: let S_i appear at position i and S_j at position j
      in compute_sccs(G), with i < j. By Tarjan's emission order
      (1972 sec-3, CLRS 22.16), there is NO edge (u in S_j,
      v in S_i) in E — any such edge would force S_i to be emitted
      AFTER S_j in Tarjan's reverse-topological natural output,
      contradicting i < j. Therefore every cross-SCC edge respects
      the position ordering: callees (smaller position) appear
      before callers (larger position).

      (P3) discharged.

    Coverage gate — corpus enumeration:

      The shipped algorithm contract is exercised empirically by
      9 test cases in compiler/ori_arc/src/graph/scc/
      tests.rs:
        Row 1: no_functions — empty graph (V = E = {})
        Row 2: single_leaf — single vertex, no edges
        Row 3: linear_chain — A -> B -> C (3 trivial SCCs,
                                           forward topological order)
        Row 4: simple_cycle — A <-> B (1 mutually-recursive
                                           SCC of size 2)
        Row 5: diamond — A -> B, A -> C, B -> D, C -> D
                                           (4 trivial SCCs, callees first)
        Row 6: self_recursive — A -> A (1 self-recursive SCC
                                           of size 1; is_recursive = true
                                           per Scc::is_recursive at
                                           mod.rs lines 42-47)
        Row 7: mixed_recursive_and_linear — mixed cycle + chain witness
        Row 8: topological_order_callees_first — explicit ordering pin:
                                           cross-SCC edges always go from
                                           earlier-position SCC to
                                           later-position SCC
        Row 9: all_functions_covered — partition completeness pin:
                                           every input function appears
                                           in exactly one emitted SCC

      Coverage axes:
        Partition (P1): Rows 1, 2, 9 (empty, singleton, exhaustive
                            partition); Row 9 is the dedicated P1 pin
        Maximality (P2): Rows 4, 6, 7 (cycle, self-loop, mixed) —
                            verify mutually-reachable vertices land in
                            the same emitted SCC
        Topological (P3): Rows 3, 5, 7, 8 (chain, diamond, mixed,
                            explicit pin) — verify cross-SCC edge
                            direction respects position ordering;
                            Row 8 is the dedicated P3 pin

      Each row's mutually-reachable equivalence-class structure is
      hand-derivable from the test fixture's `calls` / `calls_many`
      construction; the test bodies assert (a) the expected SCC
      partition (find_scc + len assertions), (b) cross-SCC edge
      direction via position lookups, and (c) is_recursive flags for
      self-loops + multi-vertex SCCs.

    Negative-direction witness — anti-monotone ordering rejected:

      Take the linear chain A -> B -> C (Row 3). The forward
      topological invariant (P3) requires emission order
      [{C}, {B}, {A}] — C has no outgoing call edges, A has no
      incoming. Reversing to [{A}, {B}, {C}] would violate (P3):
      the edge A -> B with position(A) = 0 < position(B) = 1 would
      mean a caller (A) precedes its callee (B), inverting the
      "callees before callers" invariant. IC-2/IC-3 fixpoint
      consuming sccs in this reversed order would attempt to
      compute A's MemoryContract before B's contract exists,
      violating Annex E §AIMS PL-5 "no pass relies on stale
      summaries".

      Tarjan's lowlink discipline (P2 proof) prevents this
      inversion: A's strongconnect frame cannot complete until
      every descendant (B, C) has completed and been popped.
      Therefore C is popped first, B second, A last — exactly the
      [{C}, {B}, {A}] order.

    Negative-direction witness — partition non-overlap:

      Take the diamond A -> B, A -> C, B -> D, C -> D (Row 5). The
      partition (P1) requires four singleton SCCs: {A}, {B}, {C},
      {D}. Hypothetical merge of {B} and {C} (claiming they form
      one SCC because they both descend from A and both feed into
      D) would violate (P2): B does not reach C and C does not
      reach B (no edge between them, only their common ancestor A
      and common descendant D), so {B, C} is NOT a strongly
      connected component. Tarjan's lowlink discipline correctly
      separates them — B's strongconnect frame completes (popping
      {B}) before C is even visited from A's second outgoing edge.

    Negative-direction witness — empty graph soundness:

      Take G = (V = {}, E = {}) (Row 1). compute_sccs(G) returns
      [] (the empty list). All three conjuncts hold vacuously:
        (P1) empty partition of empty V is correct (every v in {}
             appears in zero SCCs; empty quantifier over no
             vertices is true).
        (P2) no pair (u, v) in V x V exists; vacuous.
        (P3) no pair (S_i, S_j) in sccs x sccs exists; vacuous.
      The empty-graph case is the IC-7 convergence base case:
      iteration limit = 0, immediately satisfied.

    Live-variable / borrow_sources / project_alias_sources / IC-3
    contract typed pre-pass dependency: NOT REQUIRED for IC-1.
    IC-1 operates purely on the CallGraph topology (V, E) — no
    AimsStateMap lookups, no per-variable lattice state, no
    interprocedural contract values. IC-1 is the ANTECEDENT of
    IC-2/IC-3 contract computation, not a consumer of it. The
    shipped engine takes &CallGraph as its sole input
    (ori_arc::graph::scc::compute_sccs signature, mod.rs line 57).

    Cycle-soundness witness — IC-1 has no upstream dependencies:

      IC-1 is the first interprocedural rule in the AIMS pipeline
      ordering (Annex E §AIMS Step 1: analyze_program calls
      compute_sccs BEFORE any per-function intraprocedural analysis
      runs). IC-2 through IC-8a all consume IC-1's output (the
      topologically-ordered SCC list); IC-1 consumes nothing from
      the AIMS contract layer. The dependency chain is therefore
      acyclic at the proof-obligation level:
        IC-1 --> IC-2/IC-3 (param contract fixpoint per SCC)
        IC-2/IC-3 --> IC-4 (return contract join over paths)
        IC-2/IC-3 + IC-4 --> IC-5 (EffectSummary derivation)
        IC-2..IC-5 --> IC-6 (FipContract, post-realization)
        IC-2..IC-6 --> IC-7 (convergence bound)
        IC-1 --> IC-8a (address-taken / closure init)

      IC-1 is the leaf of this dependency tree.

    Algorithm-citation declaration:
      algorithm: Tarjan's strongly-connected-components
                           algorithm
      published: Tarjan, R. E., "Depth-First Search and
                           Linear Graph Algorithms", SIAM Journal
                           on Computing, vol. 1, no. 2, pp. 146-160,
                           June 1972
      complexity: O(|V| + |E|) — linear in graph size
      determinism: deterministic given a deterministic
                           iteration order over CallGraph nodes;
                           ori_arc::graph::call_graph::CallGraph
                           uses an indexmap-keyed structure to
                           preserve insertion order, so
                           compute_sccs is determinate up to
                           insertion order of ArcFunctions per
                           Annex E §AIMS PL-5 "no pass
                           relies on stale summaries" + canon.md
                           sec-1 phase purity
      reference impl: ori_arc::graph::scc::compute_sccs
                           (compiler/ori_arc/src/
                           graph/scc/mod.rs lines 48-152)
      cross-language Lean 4 src/Lean/Compiler/IR/Borrow.lean
      prior art: also uses Tarjan SCC + topological
                           order for borrow inference; cited in
                           arc.md sec-Reference Repos

    Engines dispatched:
      interprocedural_summary (PRIMARY — SCC topological ordering
        produced by Tarjan's algorithm per section_06::
        verify_ic1_scc_topological; the verifier checks all three
        conjuncts P1/P2/P3 on every emitted SCC list:
          - P1: partition completeness via union-cardinality check
            (sum of |scc.members| over all sccs == |V|) AND
            pairwise disjointness via membership set diff
          - P2: SCC maximality via reachability oracle: for every
            (u, v) in scc x scc, verify both u-reaches-v and
            v-reaches-u in the original CallGraph; for every
            cross-SCC pair, verify NOT both directions hold
          - P3: forward topological ordering via position-index
            comparison: for every edge (u, v) in E with
            scc(u) != scc(v), assert position(scc(u)) <
            position(scc(v)) in the emitted list
        emits Fail on any divergence)

  Expected:
    status: valid
    reason: section_06 interprocedural_summary engine discharges IC-1 via three-conjunct verification (P1 partition correctness + P2 SCC maximality + P3 forward topological ordering) over the 9-test corpus in ori_arc::graph::scc::tests covering empty, singleton, chain, cycle, diamond, self-loop, mixed, ordering pin, and partition pin cases; algorithm soundness inherited from Tarjan 1972 Theorem 13 + CLRS 22.16; shipped engine ori_arc::graph::scc::compute_sccs at compiler/ori_arc/src/graph/scc/mod.rs lines 48-152 implements iterative Tarjan strongconnect with O(|V| + |E|) complexity and deterministic emission order per Annex E §AIMS PL-5
